\begin{quote}
Karen Jia\hspace{0pt}\\
Submission for 2026 Stossel in the Classroom Essay Contest\hspace{0pt}\\
13 March 2026\hspace{0pt}

The Truth About Tariffs: Who Really Pays?\hspace{0pt}\\
``I'm going to buy a new car made in the USA!'' my father had
patriotically announced,\hspace{0pt}only to return with a Japanese SUV
in the end.\hspace{0pt}\\
``Dad! You promised to buy American!''\hspace{0pt}\\
``I tried,'' he replied, ``but foreign cars just made more
sense.''\hspace{0pt}\\
The truth is, American patriotism isn't affordable enough. Working and
middle-class\hspace{0pt}Americans like my family constantly have to
choose between their own American Dream and\hspace{0pt}supporting
domestic US businesses. Tariffs-- taxes on imported goods-- are intended
to help\hspace{0pt}domestic products compete against cheaper foreign
products. With a higher price placed on\hspace{0pt}foreign goods, it
seems like consumers would be more inclined to buy the
cheaper,\hspace{0pt}\\
domestically-produced alternative. Although tariffs have been successful
in the past, they did not\hspace{0pt}achieve success alone. In reality,
tariffs are a short-term solution, but not sustainable for
the\hspace{0pt}American economy in the long run.\hspace{0pt}\\
The best way to evaluate tariffs is to review historical policies such
as the Smoot-Hawley\hspace{0pt}Tariff. After WWI had damaged much of
Europe's agricultural sector, American farmers saw a\hspace{0pt}surplus
of food exports to Europe. However, by the 1920s, prices of American
farm products\hspace{0pt}began rapidly declining due to recovering
European farmers who brought increased competition.\hspace{0pt}In the
1928 presidential campaign, Herbert Hoover promised to raise tariffs on
agricultural\hspace{0pt}imports in an attempt to save the American
agricultural industry. Despite over 1,000 economists\hspace{0pt}
\end{quote}

Jia\hspace{0pt}\hspace{0pt}2\hspace{0pt}

\begin{quote}
warning against it, the Smoot-Hawley Act was signed into law on June 17,
1930 (``History of\hspace{0pt}Tariffs''). This bill increased tariffs on
more than 20,000 imported goods by 40\% to
60\%\hspace{0pt}(``Smoot-Hawley Tariff Act - Overview''). Like many
other tariff policies, its purpose was to\hspace{0pt}protect American
businesses and farmers from foreign competition. Yet, the
negative\hspace{0pt}\\
consequences far outweighed the benefits that it brought to both
domestic and foreign consumers\hspace{0pt}and businesses.\hspace{0pt}\\
Domestically speaking, the American stock market was hit hard when
foreigners began\hspace{0pt}desperately withdrawing their money because
of a sudden loss of confidence in Wall Street\hspace{0pt}following the
Stock Market Crash of 1929. For ordinary Americans, tariffs only raised
the prices\hspace{0pt}of basic necessities like food and clothing. Both
of these factors amplified the effects of the
Great\hspace{0pt}Depression.\hspace{0pt}\\
Furthermore, repercussions from the Smoot-Hawley Tariff rippled across
the globe. With\hspace{0pt}countries raising tariffs in response to each
other, international trade decreased by a total 65\%.\hspace{0pt}From
1929 to 1932, American exports dropped from \$7 billion to \$2.5
billion. Farmers were hit\hspace{0pt}especially hard, with farm exports
decreasing by one third from 1929 to 1933
(``Smoot-Hawley\hspace{0pt}Tariff Act - Overview''). This brought
additional economic instability to countries that were
just\hspace{0pt}recovering from the Great War. Thus, the Smoot-Hawley
Tariff may be partly responsible for the\hspace{0pt}rise of radical
political ideas in economically unstable nations.\hspace{0pt}\\
These historical lessons can be applied to the US-China trade conflicts
of the 21st\hspace{0pt}century. As China's economy spiked and production
costs remained lower, the US lost\hspace{0pt}\\
manufacturing jobs and fell into a deep trade deficit. American
presidents have tried tariffs as a\hspace{0pt}solution, but they
ultimately failed their protectionist goals. In 2002, Bush's Chinese
steel tariff\hspace{0pt}was faced with many retaliatory tariffs as well
as a lawsuit with the WTO. In 2009, Obama's\hspace{0pt}
